---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import charts from '../data/charts.json';
// The verify example uses the current redis chart and image, so the commands never point at a tag that is gone.
const redis = charts.find((c) => c.slug === 'redis')!;

const sections = [
  {
    h: 'Zero fixable CVEs at build',
    p: 'Every image goes through a Trivy gate that fails the build on any fixable vulnerability. Every published image is rescanned nightly and rebuilt when a fix lands. Some fixes wait on an upstream or Wolfi release; those CVEs stay open until then, and the security report lists every one.',
  },
  {
    h: 'Built from source on Wolfi',
    p: 'We compile each application from source with melange, then apko assembles a small nonroot image. There are no Dockerfiles and nothing inherited from another distro, so the attack surface is only what the app needs.',
  },
  {
    h: 'Signed, keyless',
    p: 'Each image and each chart is signed with cosign using GitHub OIDC (Sigstore). There is no long-lived key to leak. The signature ties the artifact back to the workflow that built it, and anyone can check it.',
  },
  {
    h: 'Pinned by digest',
    p: 'Charts reference images by sha256 digest, never a moving tag. What you install is exactly the artifact that passed the gate and was signed, with no room for a tag to drift underneath you.',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Security · QuenchWorks" description="How QuenchWorks gates images on fixable CVEs: built from source, signed with cosign, pinned by digest, rescanned nightly, with every open CVE published.">
  <Header />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16" data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-meta="type:Security">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ash">Security</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Trust you can verify</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ash">
      QuenchWorks is built so you never have to take our word for it. Everything below is
      reproducible and checkable from your own terminal.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 space-y-8">
      {
        sections.map((s) => (
          <section>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">{s.h}</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ash">{s.p}</p>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Verify it yourself</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ash">
        Swap in any image or chart name. These commands are real and need no key, just cosign and
        the GitHub CLI.
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-graphite p-4 font-mono text-sm text-paper"><code><span class="text-ash"># confirm an image was built and signed by our CI (images have no :latest)</span>
cosign verify ghcr.io/quenchworks/images/redis:{redis.appVersion} \
  --certificate-identity-regexp 'https://github.com/quenchworks/.+' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com

<span class="text-ash"># the same works for any chart, by its version tag</span>
<span class="text-ash"># use the plain ref: cosign rejects an oci:// prefix here</span>
cosign verify ghcr.io/quenchworks/charts/redis:{redis.chartVersion} \
  --certificate-identity-regexp 'https://github.com/quenchworks/.+' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com</code></pre>

      <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-graphite p-4 font-mono text-sm text-paper"><code><span class="text-ash"># verify the SPDX SBOM attestation and the SLSA build provenance in one step</span>
<span class="text-ash"># this is the right tool, not `cosign download sbom`</span>
gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/quenchworks/images/redis:{redis.appVersion} --owner quenchworks</code></pre>

      <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-graphite p-4 font-mono text-sm text-paper"><code><span class="text-ash"># resolve the exact sha256 digest behind the tag</span>
docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/quenchworks/images/redis:{redis.appVersion} \
  --format '{'{{.Manifest.Digest}}'}'
<span class="text-ash"># charts pin this exact digest, never a tag, so what runs cannot drift</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-line bg-graphite p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">The fixable-CVE gate</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ash">
        A Trivy scan runs on every build and hard-fails it on any fixable vulnerability, from
        CRITICAL down to LOW, with <span class="font-mono">--ignore-unfixed</span> so we only block
        on what we can actually patch. New advisories keep landing after a build, so a published image can carry CVEs
        until its rebuild, or until upstream ships a fix. The live counts, per image, are in the
        security report and at <a href="/api/v1/security.json" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-paper">/api/v1/security.json</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-line bg-graphite p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ash">
        Found something? Please follow the disclosure process in our security policy.
      </p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/quenchworks/.github/blob/main/SECURITY.md"
        class="mt-4 inline-flex rounded-lg border border-line px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-paper transition-colors hover:bg-ink"
      >
        Security policy
      </a>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
